" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-mmlab/mmdetection/v3.3.0/configs/solov2/metafile.yml)\nciteturn240view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-mmlab/mmdetection/v3.3.0/configs/solov2/metafile.yml\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 87\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-mmlab/mmdetection/v3.3.0/configs/rtmdet/metafile.yml)\nciteturn240view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-mmlab/mmdetection/v3.3.0/configs/rtmdet/metafile.yml\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 228\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-mmlab/mmdetection/v3.3.0/configs/mask_rcnn/metafile.yml)\nciteturn240view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-mmlab/mmdetection/v3.3.0/configs/mask_rcnn/metafile.yml\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 422\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAccurate segmentation of green fruit based on optimized mask RCNN application in complex orchard - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9399748/)\nciteturn240view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9399748/\",\"pattern\":\"YOLACT\"}); Total lines: 573\nL378: For the fruit with small vision, the segmentation effect is slightly worse (AP value is 33.2%), but most of the fruit can still be completely recognized, and only the objects at the boundary of the image or blurred cannot be recognized. According to cite89†Figure 8 , it can be observed that when the target fruit is covered and overlapped by branches and leaves (as shown in cite89†Figures 8F,D ), although only a small part of the target is exposed, it can still be accurately identified.\nL379: Combined with the analysis of tables and images, it can be concluded that: The distribution of prospective target fruits is dense, the target size is small, and it is easy to have large area occlusion and overlap, resulting in the serious lack of boundary information. This is the biggest difficulty in intelligent picking. However, in terms of the overall segmentation effect, the optimized mask RCNN model is ideal for the segmentation of target fruits in various complex scenes.\nL380: Even for the heavily occluded and overlapping target fruits, it still achieves high-precision and high-quality segmentation mask. Thanks to the powerful backbone network and unique post-processing module of the new model, it can better process edge information and improve resolution. Even for fruits with incomplete edge information, it can still achieve accurate positioning and high-quality segmentation.\nL381: #### Algorithm comparison\nL382: The new model takes mask RCNN as the baseline, carries out a series of optimization, and achieves high accuracy and high-quality segmentation mask. Moreover, in the process of experimental design, this study fully considered the objectivity and authenticity of experimental evaluation. Therefore, ablation experiment was adopted to fully verify that the new model has higher accuracy and higher quality for green target fruit.\nL383: In this study, the new model is compared with four algorithms: baseline mask RCNN, SOLOv2 (Wang et al., cite92†2020 ), YOLACT (Bolya et al., cite93†2019 ), and Cascade_RCNN (Cai and Vasconcelos, cite94†2019 ). Among them, mask RCNN model and Cascade_RCNN model are the two-stage instance segmentation methods, whereas SOLOv2 and YOLACT are one-stage segmentation models.\nL384: To ensure the objectivity and effectiveness of the comparative experiment, the same super parameters will be set in the experimental process, and the same persimmon dataset will be used for testing. The data results of the test are entered as shown in cite95†Table 4 .\nL385: ##### Table 4.\nL386: \nL387: Comparison of instance segmentation performance of five different networks.\nL388: Metric (%)  | mAP  | AP_{50}  | mAP_{S}  | mAP_{M}  | mAP_{L}  | mAR  | mAR_{S}  | mAR_{M}  | mAR_{L}\nL389: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL390: SOLOv2  | 58.9  | 85.1  | 18.7  | 63.2  | 68.7  | 67.9  | 29.9  | 70.7  | 78.2\nL391: Mask RCNN  | 73.2  | 90.6  | 29.6  | 75.2  | 87.6  | 77.4  | 41.6  | 79.3  | 90.3\nL392: YOLACT  | 64.6  | 88.6  | 20.0  | 67.1  | 81.2  | 72.5  | 34.5  | 74.2  | 86.9\nL393: Cascade_RCNN  | 71.4  | 90.7  | 23.9  | 72.7  | 88.5  | 75.5  | 36.9  | 77.2  | 90.6\nL394: Ours  | 76.3  | 93.0  | 33.2  | 78.4  | 90.7  | 81.1  | 45.0  | 83.3  | 92.5\nL395: cite96†Open in a new tab L396: As shown in cite95†Table 4 , the optimized mask RCNN leads other models with average recall of 81.1% and average precision of 76.3%, respectively. Compared with the baseline mask RCNN, the AP and AR values of the new model increased greatly. Moreover, the recognition rate of small target fruit in the new model is significantly higher than that in other models, especially for the recall rate.\nL397: Through further analysis of the experimental results, it is found that due to the efficient post-processing operation of BPR module, the phenomenon of repeatedly identifying overlapping multiple targets as a whole and identifying leaves as fruits is reduced in the new model. This shows that the new model reduces the false detection rate and missed detection rate to a certain extent.\nL398: The experimental data show that a series of optimization of mask RCNN greatly improves the performance of the model, which fully proves the feasibility of this research method. The dataset of this study contains a large number of complex scenes, but the model still achieves satisfactory average precision and average recall, which can meet the requirements of application to the field of agricultural intelligent picking.\nL399: As shown in cite97†Figure 9 , the segmentation effects of five different models are shown. According to the analysis of cite97†Figure 9 and cite95†Table 4 , SOLOv2 has the worst effect, the segmentation effect of the experiment is not clear, the boundary shape is incomplete, and there are a lot of missed detection. The experimental results based on the optimized mask RCNN model are the best. Both the accuracy and the quality of segmentation are better than other models.\nL400: The segmentation quality of YOLACT and Cascade_RCNN models is relatively good, but they are prone to missed detection and false detection, and the accuracy is far lower than that of the new model. The recognition accuracy of mask RCNN model is second only to the new model, but the segmentation quality of mask RCNN model is poor and the processing of edge pixels is not accurate enough. It is very easy to repeatedly recognize multiple target fruits as a whole.\nL401: This paper has important research value for the optimization of mask RCNN. At the same time, the model is optimized from the two aspects of segmentation quality and recognition accuracy, so as to realize the real high-quality accurate segmentation. Based on the performance of optimized mask RCNN model with high accuracy, strong robustness, good generalization ability, and high segmentation quality, it is of great significance to deploy to the field of intelligent agriculture.\nL402: ##### Figure 9.\nL403: \nL404: cite98†Open in a new tab L405: \nL406: Comparison of segmentation effects of five different models.\nL407: #### Ablation study\nL408: To verify the effectiveness of BPR module, this study conducted additional ablation experiments based on the persimmon dataset. In the training, the NMS threshold of BPR modules added to all models is set to 0.25. In the inference, set NMS to 0.55. HRNetV2_W18_Small is used as the optimized network of BPR module, and the size of the extracted boundary patches is 64 ^{*} 64 without padding. Then, boundary patches are resized to 256 ^{*} 256 as the input of BPR.\nL409: In this experiment, the influence of BPR module on the accuracy and recall of the model is verified by controlling the variable BPR module while keeping other settings unchanged.\nL410: This study used three different models to verify the effectiveness of BPR module, including one-stage and two-stage segmentation models. The experimental results are shown in cite99†Table 5 . By analyzing the data in cite99†Table 5 , it was found that the model with the addition of the BPR module had higher AP and AR, which could improve the recognition of target fruits at different scales.\nL411: Moreover, BPR module can be easily integrated into most pixel-based instance segmentation models and greatly improve the segmentation accuracy of the model. Thus, BPR, as a plug and play module, can easily help most models improve accuracy.\nL412: ##### Table 5.\nL413: \nL414: The effect comparison of three different models with or without BPR module.\nL415: Metric (%)  | mAP  | AP_{50}  | mAP_{S}  | mAP_{M}  | mAP_{L}  | mAR  | mAR_{S}  | mAR_{M}  | mAR_{L}\nL416: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL417: SOLO  | 37.8  | 60  | 5.3  | 36.6  | 62.1  | 49.4  | 10.4  | 49.2  | 72.1\nL418: SOLO^{+}  | 39.7  | 62.3  | 7.1  | 39  | 64.5  | 53  | 12.7  | 51.2  | 74.6\nL419: D2Det  | 58  | 83.2  | 40.6  | 65  | 87.8  | 61.9  | 45.9  | 69.1  | 89.6\nL420: D2Det^{+}  | 61.1  | 86  | 43.3  | 67.9  | 90.1  | 65.1  | 48.8  | 72  | 92.7\nL446: ## Publisher's note\nL447: \nL448: All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their affiliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher.\nL449: ## References\nL450:   1. Bai T., Pang Y., Wang J., Han K., Luo J., Wang H., et al. (2020). An Optimized Faster R-CNN Method Based on DRNet and RoI Align for Building Detection in Remote Sensing Images. J. Rem. Sens. 12, 762. 10.3390/rs12050762 [cite101†DOI†doi.org ] [cite102†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com ]\nL451:   2. Bargoti S., Underwood J. (2017). Deep fruit detection in orchards. IEEE Int. Conf. Robot. Autom. 3626–3633. 10.1109/ICRA.2017.7989417 [cite103†DOI†doi.org ] [cite104†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com ]\nL452:   3. Bhargava A., Bansal A. (2020). Automatic detection and grading of multiple fruits by machine learning[J]. Food Anal. Meth. 13, 751–761. 10.1007/s12161-019-01690-6 [cite105†DOI†doi.org ] [cite106†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com ]\nL453:   4. Bird J. J., Barnes C. M., Manso L. J., Ekárt A., Faria D. (2022). Fruit quality and defect image classification with conditional GAN data augmentation. J. Scientia Horticulturae. 293, 110684. 10.1016/j.scienta.2021.110684 [cite107†DOI†doi.org ] [cite108†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com ]\nL454:   5. Bolya D., Zhou C., Xiao F., Lee Y. (2019). Yolact: Real-time instance segmentation. Proc. IEEE/CVF Int. Conf. Comput. Vision. 9157–9166. 10.1109/ICCV.2019.00925 [cite109†DOI†doi.org ] [cite110†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ] [cite111†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com ]\nL455:   6. Cai Z., Vasconcelos N. (2019). Cascade R-CNN: high quality object detection and instance segmentation. J. IEEE Transac. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell. 43, 1483–1498. 10.1109/TPAMI.2019.2956516 [cite112†DOI†doi.org ] [cite113†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ] [cite114†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com ]\nL456:   7. Chen M., Tang Y., Zou X., Huang Z., Zhou H., Chen S. (2021), 3D. global mapping of large-scale unstructured orchard integrating eye-in-hand stereo vision SLAM. Comput. Electron. Agricult. 187, 